ou learn from it and and and I  think having some humor about it too  sometimes is you know when you can when  it's something like that where you can  just say let's just laugh and move on  no and I have to say it takes an  incredible security to bring up you know  if you ask me to bring up and I have  them my major failures over the last  year three years ten years  there's a part of my brain says Sal  don't bring it up then people are going  to know you know all your weaknesses  takes a lot to to bring that up uh but I  really appreciate that we have another  question from Imani  I was just curious what strong women  have inspired or in  or into you to become the powerful  leader that you are today  who has inspired me well many people  along the way my mom definitely inspired  me  and as a kid I was always really  impressed with Marie Curie and she was  my icon as a as a kid she is someone who  holds two Nobel prizes and I don't think  there are a lot of other people who have  achieved that in different fields and  her daughter also has a Nobel Prize  which I always thought was really  impressive so uh she was my one of my  icons and  but I would also say that a lot of the  people who mentored me and helped me get  to where I am were not always they were  not always women in fact most of the  time they were men because most of the  people in positions above me at at  Google  um in Tech were men and and there were a  lot of super supportive uh mentors that  I had and I I always remind people like  whether they're a mentee or a mentor  that anyone like anyone regardless of  their backgrounds you can mentor someone  they don't have to look like you or be  like you you can just uh find someone  who is talented that that you want to  mentor and and you're again your mentors  can be different from the backgrounds  you came from and they can be really  helpful to you so I'm very thankful for  all the people who mentored me along the  way even though they came from different  backgrounds than I did and they  definitely helped me and I definitely  learned from them  absolutely and you know I know I know  we're running low on time I could talk  to you for hours there's so many  questions I have about your life about  YouTube I am you know we touched on the  subject of learning obviously Khan  Academy is very focused on learning and  YouTube has been a big part of that that  Journey when I was making content from  my cousins and and still do I made three  videos that I upload onto YouTube  yesterday so I'm still very active there  what's your what's your what's your  dream if you if you fast forward 5 10 20  years for  you know I don't know if you think in  terms of Legacy but you know the impact  that you want to have through YouTube  so we definitely think about YouTube and  how we can continue to grow it and I'm  always just amazed of all the new topics  and new ways that we can enable voices  around the world to come and to share  their story and so part of our goal is  to enable the next generation of  storytellers and to help them be able to  to do that and to make a business and  the flip side of that is is that we can  have incredibly compelling content for  people around the world on any topic and  so if you look at if you look at YouTube  I see that we bring mini value and of  course like enabling all the fun videos  and music videos but we also deal with a  lot of really important topics that just  were not talked about before  um whether that involves mental health  issues or hearing from people of  different backgrounds it never were  represented It On Media traditionally  like those are really compelling and  important points of view and just you  know what one last question it feels  like there's two competing wins there is  you know one wind where there's more  tools and resources YouTube Khan Academy  you know all the other things that  Google provides that are enabling the  individual to publish to express  themselves to discover points of view to  learn it really does feel like we're an  inflection point in a Renaissance so to  speak in Human Society on the other side  there are reasons why people can get  cynical it feels like we are more  polarized as a country or as a world it  feels like you know inequity is growing  it feels like people aren't listening to  each other anymore you know the  climate's in trouble  if there's a young person watching and  and there's a pandemic and and people  are feeling isolated and lonely  what what message can you can you give  to someone who's kind of trying to  navigate these these two currents  YouTube does enable people to connect  with each other as humans and there's  something very human about the fact that  people are a lot of times going to  YouTube and talking about how they feel  what their deepest challenges were about  diseases they have and there's something  really cathartic about people going and  sharing the challenges that they have  and we've seen other people really be  able to relate and benefit from that so  I do see a human connection that's  incredibly powerful on YouTube for us to  understand each other more as humans and  I've also seen that there are there's an  incredible amount of good that comes  whether it's something like  um the the 20 million trees that are  being planted  um and organized on YouTube or  fundraisers to support people during  hard times and so I do see that YouTube  can be used for all of kinds of good  outcomes and I encourage you to use like  you know YouTube and all social  platforms if there's a cause you believe  in you can make a difference you can  make a video you can organize groups and  I encourage you to speak your voice and  now is the best time ever to be able to  do that so I encourage you to do what's  important for you and to speak up  now thank you so much for that I'm  inspired I'm gonna go use some social  media to try to try to change the world  well Susan thank you so much I know  you're incredibly busy and thank you for  making the time out for all of us and  all the students who are watching and  their questions uh and uh you know I I  I'm excited to be part of your journey  uh which you you are empowering Khan  Academy on so many levels uh and and see  where where we can take the world  oh thank you and I'm and I'm glad we  have been part of your journey too  thanks everyone for joining as uh you  could tell you know these conversations  uh when you have a guest like Susan I  could I could talk for hours so much to  learn from and I find her to be  incredibly inspiring and you know I've  I've known Susan and her family for many  years now uh and uh you know every time  I chat uh it's I never cease to be  impressed by uh what actually her and  her sisters and her mother are  accomplishing on a regular basis so uh  thank you all for joining we do have  we're actually going to have a our next  show is March 10th the guest is Mark  Sternberg it'll actually be hosted by  Khan Academy's Chief learning officer  Kristen deserbo and they're going to be  talking this is a mark Sternberg from  the Walton family foundation and we'll  talk about philanthropy and learning and  impact on education so look forward to  seeing all of you all there uh have a  good weekend
 

